\details{
This step detects peaks in measurement data and creates a new \code{.peaks}
column containing the detected peaks for each sample. The original
\code{.measures} column is preserved.

\strong{Detection algorithms:}
\itemize{
\item \code{"prominence"} (default): Finds local maxima and calculates their prominence
(the height above the surrounding contour). Use \code{min_prominence} to
exclude maxima below the chosen prominence threshold.
\item \code{"derivative"}: Finds peaks by detecting zero-crossings in the first
derivative. Noise can introduce additional zero-crossings.
\item \code{"local_maxima"}: Finds local maxima above the chosen height threshold,
including maxima caused by noise.
\item \code{"cwt"}: Links Mexican-hat wavelet maxima across widths to identify
persistent peaks. Requires a uniformly spaced axis. See
\link{peak_detection_cwt} for widths, local SNR, missing data, and limits.
}

Additional algorithms can be registered by technique packs using
\code{\link[=register_peak_algorithm]{register_peak_algorithm()}}.

The detection thresholds are tunable with \code{\link[=peak_min_height]{peak_min_height()}},
\code{\link[=peak_min_distance]{peak_min_distance()}}, \code{\link[=peak_min_prominence]{peak_min_prominence()}}, and
\code{\link[=peak_snr_threshold]{peak_snr_threshold()}}. The default search ranges are only starting points:
height and prominence use signal units, distance uses x-axis units, and SNR
uses MAD units. Adjust the ranges to the measurement scale. Prominence is
offered as a tunable threshold only for the built-in prominence algorithm.
For the built-in CWT detector, use, for example,
\code{algorithm_params = list(min_snr = tune::tune("ridge_snr"))} to search
over wavelet ridge SNR. A grid column named \code{ridge_snr} supplies the
